From 9691928902abec7cfbd7d56be77b60c9a61f2747 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:55:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(comparison): the before/after shows itself, and six
 services get a visual
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A comparison slider parked at 52% is a photograph with a line through it.
Most visitors never touch it, so most visitors never see the comparison —
which is the only reason the component exists.

It now sweeps slowly between 22% and 78% on a sine easing, decelerating at
each end where the eye needs a beat to read the difference, and hands control
over the instant anyone reaches for it. Motion that cannot be stopped is
worse than no motion, so the drift stops while a pointer is over it or focus
is inside it, for 5s after any drag or key press, when scrolled out of view,
when the tab is hidden, and entirely under prefers-reduced-motion. The
control is still a real range input, so arrow keys work and it is operable
with no pointer.

Placed inside the estimator under the controls, swapping with the Service
dropdown — choose "Repair & restoration" and you see repair work while the
repair band is still on screen. It is the one place on the site where the
money and the craft are in the same glance. Also on all 8 service pages,
above the coverage map. Left off the compact homepage strip, which exists to
move someone to /estimate rather than to hold them.

Six services were asked for. There is ONE pair in the repository, and its two
frames are not the same room: before-worn.jpg is a bare floor by a window,
after-refinished.jpg is a hallway with a staircase. Two unrelated renders
either side of a divider.

Pairing renders behind a Before/After label on all six would have looked
exactly like what was asked for and would be manufacturing evidence — two
frames either side of a divider assert that THIS floor became THAT floor, and
no caption softens it. That is the invented-testimonial mistake with a slider
attached, and this site has already paid for that lesson once.

So data/comparisons.ts gives every service a real visual and marks which are
comparisons. Refinishing renders in the slider, labelled a rendering. The
other five render as a single photograph with one line naming the frame that
is missing — "the carpeted flight before, and the finished flight from the
same step after" — which is more persuasive than a fake pair and costs
nothing to be right about. tests/comparisons.test.ts fails the build if a
pair is marked verified while pointing at any file in the known AI-generated
set, so setting the flag honestly is a one-line change the day the
photographs exist.

Blocker #12 is the deliverable: twelve photographs, a phone, four rules —
same position, same height, same light, and take the before even when the job
looks boring. Repair first, because the whole claim of that service is that
the repair disappears and a slider is the only way to prove it.

Also fixes a misrouted lead, found because putting the comparison in the
estimator put the lead form in the same screenshot for the first time. The
summary read "Sanding & refinishing" while the submitted Service select read
"New hardwood install". Confirmed in a browser: set the estimator to deck and
the submitted field stayed install. defaultValue is applied once at mount and
ignored after, so configuring the estimator BEFORE the form mounted worked —
the path anyone testing would take — and changing it afterwards silently did
not. Service, city and size are controlled now and re-seed when the estimator
moves, with a manual edit still winning until it moves again.

327 tests pass (313 before). 393 pages, audit clean. Browser-verified: drifts
unattended 64 -> 78 -> 60 over 3.2s, holds on hover, hands over on drag,
completely still under reduced motion. 5 routes x 4 viewports, no overflow.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
